+      docs/metrics_context_model.md, docs/site_architecture.md. Two further `continental_club`
+      mentions are deliberately NOT rewritten — product_direction_threads.md:71 is a dated
+      snapshot ("registry state as of 2026-05-25") and :88 uses it as a hypothetical folder name
+      in a 2026-05-27 decision record. Editing either would falsify a record of what was true.
+
+  (2) NAMING — the second new club type.
+      CPO ANSWER, verbatim: "it should be intercontinental_super_cup".
+      So the type is `intercontinental_super_cup` while `label_en` stays "Intercontinental Cup":
+      the type takes the super-cup naming, the label stays the competition's actual name, which
+      is what a reader sees.
+
+  (3) FORM WINDOW for both new club types. Each inherits an existing row rather than getting a
+      new rule: `club_world_cup` follows `continental_cup`; `intercontinental_super_cup` follows
+      `continental_super_cup` — so it shows last-5 before, and nothing during or after, because a
+      super cup is a single match. (The CPO's first phrasing sent both to `continental_cup` and he
+      corrected the second one the same day; only the corrected ruling stands.)
+      Recorded in docs/metrics_context_model.md §4, which is the ruling surface for form windows.
+      ⚠ THE LOAD-BEARING CONSEQUENCE: the cumulative within-tournament window stays NATIONAL-ONLY.
+      A club competition never gets it however tournament-shaped it looks, so the Club World Cup
+      takes last-5 exactly like the Champions League. That is also why CWC's re-type moves no
+      number — it resolved to last-5 as `continental_club` and still does.
+
+  (4) KNOCKOUT COMPETITIONS HAVE NO "AFTER IT'S FINISHED" WINDOW. CPO, verbatim: "for club
+      qualifying and for domestic cups there only the pre match, during but no after it's
+      finished." So `domestic_cup` and `club_qualifying` both take "—" in that column, joining
+      the three super cups and the two friendlies. `domestic_cup` previously read "Their full cup
+      run" and `club_qualifying` read "Last 5 across all the club's competitions" — the only cell
+      in the matrix that put a live-form rule in a completed-competition column. Both struck.
+      THE TEST, ruled the same day when the builder asked whether `continental_cup` had the same
+      problem: an "after" figure exists when every entrant plays a GROUP OR LEAGUE PHASE, i.e.
+      when there are enough matches to be worth aggregating. CPO: "no champions league has group
+      phase -> enough matches, so keep full completed edition (or all available matches from that
+      competition)". So `continental_cup` and `club_world_cup` KEEP it — a club knocked out in the
+      round of 16 still has a full group campaign behind it — while a pure knockout does not,
+      because a team's run there can be one match and phase is per team (§5 below).
+      ⚠ AND THE PHRASE IS CLARIFIED: "full completed edition" means ALL OF THAT TEAM'S MATCHES in
+      that edition, not "only if they reached the final". Recorded in §4, because that reading is
+      the entire reason the knockout rows are empty and the group-phase rows are not.
+      ⚠ The builder filled `club_qualifying` with "Their full qualifying run" before this ruling
+      and then reported "no cell in the matrix changed", which was wrong twice over. Corrected.
+      ⚠ Underlying observation, noted and NOT acted on: "After it's finished" is not a form window
+      at all — a finished competition has no next match, so there is nothing to compute. That
+      column belongs to the SEASON window, the other of the two. The matrix carries both windows
+      in one table without saying so, which is why its edge cells stop making sense.
+
+  (5) PHASE DETECTION is per TEAM, not per competition. CPO: "domestic_cup as well, some teams
+      may be out after the first round." §3 said phases are detected "by fixture counts in the
+      current edition" without saying WHOSE fixtures. For a league it makes no difference; for a
+      knockout it decides everything — a club eliminated in round one has 0 upcoming fixtures in
+      that cup from that moment, so it is in "after it's finished" while the cup runs on for
+      months. Clarified in §3. No cell in the matrix changes; the ambiguity was in the detection
+      rule above it, and "their full cup run" is only honest once the phase is the team's.
+      ⚠ NOT verified: whether the models actually count the team's fixtures or the competition's.
+      That is the same class as the two divergences below and needs the same check.
+
